The `@nx/webpack:ssr-dev-server` and `@nx/rspack:ssr-dev-server` executors are deprecated and will
be removed in Nx 25.
They built the browser bundle and the server bundle in watch mode, started the server, and waited
for its port.
Nx can do each of those with continuous tasks.
The `@nx/js/tsx` plugin runs `server.ts` from source with [`tsx`](https://tsx.is/), so the server
needs no build of its own.

## Run the server from source

`@nx/js` doesn't install `tsx`, so add it to the workspace's dev dependencies:

```shell
npm install -D tsx@^4.21.1
```

`tsx` 4.21.0 and several earlier versions don't restart a CommonJS server when a file it imports
changes.
This happens on some Node.js 24 and 26 releases.

Add a shebang and a target name to the top of `server.ts`.
Resolve the browser bundle from the project folder, and report readiness once the server listens:

```ts {% title="apps/my-app/server.ts" %}
#!/usr/bin/env -S tsx --tsconfig tsconfig.app.json
// @nx-target-name: serve-ssr

// ...the rest of the server

const browserDist = path.join(process.cwd(), '../../dist/apps/my-app/browser');

const server = app.listen(port, async () => {
  console.log(`Listening on http://localhost:${port}`);
  // Set when Nx waits for this server to be ready
  if (process.env.NX_READY_SIGNAL_SOCKET) {
    try {
      // Function keeps TypeScript and bundlers from rewriting or bundling the import
      const load = new Function('s', 'return import(s)') as (
        s: string
      ) => Promise<{ emitReadySignal(server: unknown): boolean }>;
      (await load('@nx/js/ready-signal')).emitReadySignal(server);
    } catch {
      // @nx/js isn't installed
    }
  }
});
```

`--tsconfig` gives `tsx` the JSX settings of `tsconfig.app.json`.
`tsx` applies a tsconfig only to the files it includes, and the app's `tsconfig.json` includes
none.
Without the flag, the server fails to render with `React is not defined`.
`tsx` runs the server from the project folder, so the browser bundle path is relative to it.
The server imports `@nx/js`, so the root `package.json` has to list it.
To add it, see [waiting for the server to be ready](/docs/technologies/node/introduction#waiting-for-the-server-to-be-ready).

The name `serve-ssr` keeps the target apart from the `serve` target that `@nx/webpack/plugin` and
`@nx/rspack/plugin` infer for the browser dev server.

Register the plugin in `nx.json`.
It runs scripts in watch mode by default, so the server restarts when its files change:

```json {% title="nx.json" %}
{
  "plugins": ["@nx/js/tsx"]
}
```

To limit the plugin to one app, scope it with `include`, for example `apps/my-app/{package.json,project.json,server.ts}`.
Keep the app's `package.json` or `project.json` in the pattern: the plugin finds projects through the markers that match.

## Build the browser bundle alongside

In the project configuration, replace the `serve`, `serve-browser`, `server`, and `serve-server`
targets that used the executors.
Add a continuous target that rebuilds the browser bundle, and make `serve-ssr` wait for its first
build:

```json {% title="apps/my-app/project.json" %}
{
  "targets": {
    "build-watch": {
      "continuous": true,
      "command": "webpack-cli build --watch",
      "options": {
        "cwd": "{projectRoot}",
        "env": { "NODE_ENV": "development" }
      },
      "readyWhen": { "logMatches": "compiled" }
    },
    "serve-ssr": {
      "dependsOn": [{ "target": "build-watch", "waitFor": "ready" }],
      "readyWhen": { "signal": true }
    }
  }
}
```

With Rspack, the command is `rspack build --watch --node-env=development`, and `env` isn't needed.
webpack-cli 7 doesn't accept `--node-env`, so the webpack target sets `NODE_ENV` through `env`.
`readyWhen` marks `build-watch` ready at the first line with `compiled`, which webpack and Rspack
print when a build finishes, with or without errors.
`serve-ssr` starts after that first build, so the browser bundle exists when the server reads it.
If the first build takes longer than a minute, raise `timeout` in `readyWhen`, in milliseconds.
`readyWhen` on `serve-ssr` makes tasks that wait for it, such as e2e tasks, start once the server
calls `emitReadySignal`.
They get its address in `NX_READY_URL`.

Run `nx serve-ssr my-app` to start everything.

## Limits

`tsx` compiles TypeScript and JSX, but not the CSS, CSS module, and image imports that only the
bundler handles.
If the server renders components that import those, it can't run from source.

For new server-side rendered apps, use a framework with its own Nx plugin, such as
[Next.js](/docs/technologies/react/next/introduction) or [React Router](/docs/kb/react-router).
